--TEST--
Of the members ADR 0079 § 12 makes deterministic, the readers answer from the host outside a test and only `Core\Test::advance` refuses -- ADR 0079 § 12
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 12 names `Core\Time::now`, `Core\Uuid::v7` and `Core\Random::int` as what a
// test's `at:` and `seed:` fix, and `Core\Test::advance` as the mutator. One
// question asked of all four -- "what happens with nothing fixed?" -- asserting
// that they *agree* on which of them is the mutator, rather than that each
// answered something plausible on its own line.
//
// The reason this is worth pinning: every one of the three readers was routed
// through a new seam to make § 12 work (`nvs_stdlib::time::wall_clock` and
// `nvs_stdlib::random::draw`), and the failure that seam invites is a reader
// that starts refusing, or answering a frozen value, where no test declared
// anything.

use Core\Test;
use Core\Time;
use Core\Random;
use Core\Uuid;

int $answered = 0;
try {
    Time::now();
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $a) {
}
try {
    Uuid::v7();
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $b) {
}
try {
    Random::int(1, 6);
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $c) {
}
echo "answered=", $answered, "/3\n";

int $refused = 0;
try {
    Core\Test::advance(2h);
} catch (LogicError $d) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}
echo "refused=", $refused, "/1\n";

// And the readers keep answering afterwards: the refused mutation left nothing
// half-written on the context it was reaching for, which is the other thing a
// mutator that failed partway would break.
$answered = 0;
try {
    Time::now();
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $e) {
}
try {
    Uuid::v7();
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $f) {
}
try {
    Random::int(1, 6);
    $answered = $answered + 1;
} catch (Throwable $g) {
}
echo "answered=", $answered, "/3\n";
--EXPECT--
answered=3/3
refused=1/1
answered=3/3
